---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Methodology"
  description="How China Manufacturing Intelligence verifies facts, labels claims, and sources evidence."
  canonicalPath="/methodology/"
>
  <section class="hero">
    <h1>Methodology</h1>
    <p>
      How we research, verify, and label claims — and why you should trust the
      <strong> FACT</strong> / <strong> INFERENCE</strong> / <strong> OPINION</strong> markers.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="prose-page">
    <h2>Claim labeling</h2>
    <p>Every substantive claim on this site is labeled one of three ways:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <span class="fact fact--FACT">FACT</span>
        A verifiable statement backed by at least one primary or strong secondary source
        (government, customs, industry association, company filing, or established trade press).
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="fact fact--INFERENCE">INFERENCE</span>
        A conclusion we draw from verified facts — not directly sourced, but derived from
        sourced evidence and stated as reasoning, not as an established fact.
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="fact fact--OPINION">OPINION</span>
        An editorial judgment or recommendation, explicitly ours and not presented as fact.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Evidence hierarchy</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <strong>Primary</strong> — government publications (MIIT, customs, provincial/city
        government), company annual reports and filings, industry association data.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Strong secondary</strong> — established industry press, academic research,
        financial-regulatory disclosures.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Discovery only</strong> — B2B platforms and blogs; used only to surface
        leads for further verification, never as the basis of a factual claim.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Hard rules</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Never write from memory when verification is possible.</li>
      <li>Never invent manufacturers, locations, markets, production numbers, rankings, market share, or certifications.</li>
      <li>Never convert \u201Csupplier\u201D into \u201Cmanufacturer\u201D, or \u201Clocated in a city\u201D into \u201Cfactory there\u201D.</li>
      <li>Quantity is secondary to information density. We prefer a smaller set of well-evidenced pages over a large set of thin ones.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Quality gate</h2>
    <p>
      Before publication, each major page must score <strong>≥8/10</strong> on research depth,
      evidence quality, and originality. Pages below threshold are returned to research, not
      published.
    </p>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
